[{:db/id "q-48102/selection/92861585f929682e1983ae5c728db701972383d1b14a551ffdf0b5ac5f848be0" :answer/id #uuid "a0ea26bc-8a4f-54b8-a034-57df54c96c0c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(2)$ does not exist" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48102/selection/fd6426755d22f4317347219d3d99e80396aad25e3c4556d207fd2f29ecf4a12a" :answer/id #uuid "60d96c7c-bb13-5920-a2f0-e2b60689d2ed" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48102/selection/8bb1b1393896e749c376b11abd237d96fb660d550bd060e1f0467570ae6af2d9" :answer/id #uuid "32cd595f-f3df-511f-8fc4-c884f5a4245b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48102/selection/cf93754e682e9ae90e30f7712faafc2f53dcf10208fb2ff2c79e1c9a7c3de976" :answer/id #uuid "aadc80df-023b-57fc-9851-53874e16937a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48102/selection/bfafb0345295d7a1f5de847f7fe1a32272dd270e2a859c61d9bd38f210dbce17" :answer/id #uuid "960de3b2-f53d-58c4-aca7-321be1ad9945" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48102/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "033ea7c7-58e8-5df4-a0d8-18ff2aaba1b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-48102/selection/bfafb0345295d7a1f5de847f7fe1a32272dd270e2a859c61d9bd38f210dbce17" :answer-field/choices ["q-48102/selection/92861585f929682e1983ae5c728db701972383d1b14a551ffdf0b5ac5f848be0" "q-48102/selection/fd6426755d22f4317347219d3d99e80396aad25e3c4556d207fd2f29ecf4a12a" "q-48102/selection/8bb1b1393896e749c376b11abd237d96fb660d550bd060e1f0467570ae6af2d9" "q-48102/selection/cf93754e682e9ae90e30f7712faafc2f53dcf10208fb2ff2c79e1c9a7c3de976" "q-48102/selection/bfafb0345295d7a1f5de847f7fe1a32272dd270e2a859c61d9bd38f210dbce17"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-48102" :question/id #uuid "16483a30-6f65-5f41-998f-ed4512f89b1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-48102" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=2?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}, & x<2 \\\\ 4, & x=2 \\\\ -2x, & x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(2)=4,$ so $f(2)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-4, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(-2x) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-4$ $✓$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-4,$ while $f(2)=4.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$ because ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-48102/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-48102"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-104129/selection/ab18f4396373d7e8cd2942e5d92c9b05f63393a023af463ce3b482034c50ec5c" :answer/id #uuid "956020a8-0cca-5229-8c7d-f27a91b81b6f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(3)$  does not exist" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104129/selection/47d63e4b4f763d926db2ce066f03c80b89df2d6b1b0d0a075c65dfd8c5f2eaa9" :answer/id #uuid "ec6185fa-5c73-5586-a0a9-4e4ccb03476d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104129/selection/57b402b7f4b9218630f136f0f020cd2c767a4c33d229d5fcf8136ae9aa4acdae" :answer/id #uuid "72f66ccf-ef41-54c4-ad39-c6cc4b52a20d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104129/selection/c7a382ca4ddf50e50994425dfadf5b5da55511161e17259f0aae6b8ef81c108d" :answer/id #uuid "37e8b8c6-05f9-5f9a-a137-d6f5fac9db4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne 3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104129/selection/8005381eb3320824ae354e3163d0d45253c5ba2a68bce64b69e76c8b572607e7" :answer/id #uuid "49a8b7af-e52a-57ae-8700-b3706d8389db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)>0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104129/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5c8f2adf-6c82-5526-81db-8a54996ad34a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-104129/selection/ab18f4396373d7e8cd2942e5d92c9b05f63393a023af463ce3b482034c50ec5c" :answer-field/choices ["q-104129/selection/ab18f4396373d7e8cd2942e5d92c9b05f63393a023af463ce3b482034c50ec5c" "q-104129/selection/47d63e4b4f763d926db2ce066f03c80b89df2d6b1b0d0a075c65dfd8c5f2eaa9" "q-104129/selection/57b402b7f4b9218630f136f0f020cd2c767a4c33d229d5fcf8136ae9aa4acdae" "q-104129/selection/c7a382ca4ddf50e50994425dfadf5b5da55511161e17259f0aae6b8ef81c108d" "q-104129/selection/8005381eb3320824ae354e3163d0d45253c5ba2a68bce64b69e76c8b572607e7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104129" :question/id #uuid "2ecb21d1-c065-5b6a-a4bd-5b61570cf37e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104129" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=3?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{x-3}, & x<3 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{x-3}, & x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=3.$\n\n- We have that $f(3)$ is not defined since $x=3$ does not belong to the domain of $f(x).$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=3$ because $f(3)$ does not exist." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-104129/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104129"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342128/field-1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "6736a8c4-83e7-5a08-8122-a5fa3569f09c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342128/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "76626e07-2f2f-5702-b3fa-1a865730c2ec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342128/field-1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer-field/choices ["q-342128/field-1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342128" :question/id #uuid "7dfe1c04-ab2a-5512-a89e-4792c29fa3e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-342128" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-\\frac{3}{5}$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {(-3/5)}^{-}}f(x)=7,$ then\n\n$f(-\\frac{3}{5})=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-\\frac{3}{5}.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to (-3/5)}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf(-\\frac{3}{5})={lim}_{x\\to (-3/5)}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {(-3/5)}^{-}}f(x)=7.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342128/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342128"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342241/field-1/f509030681214332b5989836fe0af4b2eb5858d193e689fd8441dff5cd45ff45" :answer/id #uuid "945df1d3-7212-563c-b240-9ce35c9979be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342241/field-1/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "48016568-2b77-5d3b-afa6-a9cf880bab44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342241/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "92744f46-5054-52de-9c8e-4597c808d422" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342241/field-1/f509030681214332b5989836fe0af4b2eb5858d193e689fd8441dff5cd45ff45" :answer-field/choices ["q-342241/field-1/f509030681214332b5989836fe0af4b2eb5858d193e689fd8441dff5cd45ff45" "q-342241/field-1/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342241" :question/id #uuid "04895225-82a3-50b3-9415-2a00cdf9e287" :question/math-academy-id "q-342241" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3$ and $f(3)=-3,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{x+(f(x))^{3}}{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3)=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{x+(f(x))^{3}}{2} & =\\frac{3+(-3)^{3}}{2}=-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342241/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342241"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342244/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "93493554-3828-527c-bbd2-eb64b57ecf5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342244/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "8d267e84-08f3-52b8-af3a-00d7b8f9ba73" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342244/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-342244/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342244" :question/id #uuid "e227b23a-bc8f-5586-a176-238c4b7efefa" :question/math-academy-id "q-342244" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$ and $f(-2)=2,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}({x}^{3}+2(f(x))^{2})=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=f(-2)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}({x}^{3}+2(f(x))^{2}) & =(-2)^{3}+2(-2)^{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342244/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342244"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
